; DERIVED, not verbatim. Written by this library: the W3C Recommendation
; Securing Verifiable Credentials using JOSE and COSE
; (REC-vc-jose-cose-20250515) prints no ABNF. Source
; .cache/w3c-vc-jose-cose.bin, sha256
; 6c70cf8d7de9d8a3ff28a743fea9d569a67a402111c3245fdafbaa2ad6709af9;
; quotations of it are checked against .cache/w3c-vc-jose-cose.txt, sha256
; f02bd4966800863d533746c6c39ad267ada1b8bfcbb92186c204b617bb46c126, and
; quotations of other documents against the copies README.md lists.
; Notation: ABNF, RFC 5234. Quoted literals are case-insensitive (RFC 5234
; section 2.3), which is intended: the URI scheme and media type names
; compare case-insensitively (RFC 6838 section 4.2 for media types; inferred
; for the scheme). Core rules ALPHA, DIGIT and HEXDIG are RFC 5234 Appendix
; B.1's. This file is ASCII, as RFC 5234 comments must be, so section signs
; are spelled out and quotation marks are straight. Lines end in LF, not the
; CRLF of RFC 5234's own grammar.
;
; WHAT IT IS. The data: URL that is the id of an
; EnvelopedVerifiableCredential or EnvelopedVerifiablePresentation, for the
; six media types this specification registers. VC-DATA-MODEL-2.0 says "The
; id value of the object MUST be a data: URL [RFC2397]" (VC-DATA-MODEL-2.0
; section 4.13), imported because the source requires the type defined
; there: "Verifiable Credentials secured in verifiable presentations MUST
; use the Enveloped Verifiable Credential type defined by the
; [VC-DATA-MODEL-2.0]." (w3c-vc-jose-cose#R-0021) and its twins. Section
; 8.4, Data URIs, prints three such URLs as Examples 21 to 23 and states no
; rule.
;
; TOP RULES: vc-jwt-data-uri, vp-jwt-data-uri, vc-sd-jwt-data-uri,
; vp-sd-jwt-data-uri, vc-cose-data-uri, vp-cose-data-uri; and dataurl, RFC
; 2397 section 3, for any media type. A data: URL with another media type is
; not excluded by any MUST of the source (w3c-vc-jose-cose#R-0011 concerns
; an implementation that does not know which to use); ABNF cannot say any
; media type except these six, so that case is
; enveloped.derived.schema.json's #data-url. The six rules admit media
; type parameters (attribute=value), as RFC 2397's mediatype does. Each
; section 6.1 template lists none (Required parameters: N/A and Optional
; parameters: N/A, inline.verbatim.txt I-01 to I-06), but section 6 is
; marked non-normative, so it grounds no rule here.
;
; Meant to agree with enveloped.derived.schema.json #data-uri-vc and
; #data-uri-vp, which add one rule ABNF cannot state (a credential's URL may
; not carry a presentation media type, and the reverse): README.md, Checks,
; runs both on every data: URL the examples contain and on the reject list,
; and reports every disagreement.

; application/vc+jwt, application/vp+jwt: a JWT in the JWS Compact
; Serialization, three non-empty base64url segments, or the base64 encoding
; of one (RFC 2397 permits it; the source neither shows nor forbids it).
; Inferred from section 5.1, which is normative and takes vc+jwt and vp+jwt
; as an input document secured as a JWT [RFC7519] (inline.verbatim.txt I-08;
; the section 6.1.1 and 6.1.2 registrations of a JSON Web Token say the same,
; in a section marked non-normative, and ground nothing), from RFC 7519
; section 1, "JWTs are always represented using the JWS
; Compact Serialization or the JWE Compact Serialization." (RFC 7519 section
; 1) from w3c-vc-jose-cose#R-0012, which makes the JWT a JWS, and from
; w3c-vc-jose-cose#R-0025. Every segment is non-empty: an empty signature is
; the unsecured form, which w3c-vc-jose-cose#R-0005 and
; w3c-vc-jose-cose#R-0023 exclude ("is used to communicate that a Verifiable
; Credential or Verifiable Presentation encoded as a JWT Claims Set has no
; integrity protection." (section 1.1.2.1)). A JWE (five segments) matches
; nothing here.
vc-jwt-data-uri    = "data:application/vc+jwt" *( ";" parameter ) ( "," jws-compact / ";base64," base64-data )
vp-jwt-data-uri    = "data:application/vp+jwt" *( ";" parameter ) ( "," jws-compact / ";base64," base64-data )

; application/vc+sd-jwt, application/vp+sd-jwt: an SD-JWT or SD-JWT+KB in
; the compact serialization (w3c-vc-jose-cose#R-0042, imported through
; w3c-vc-jose-cose#R-0027 and its twins), or its base64 encoding. The
; source's own words: "If the KB-JWT is not present, the SD-JWT must end
; with a tilde (~) character." (section 1.1.2.2)
vc-sd-jwt-data-uri = "data:application/vc+sd-jwt" *( ";" parameter ) ( "," sd-jwt-compact / ";base64," base64-data )
vp-sd-jwt-data-uri = "data:application/vp+sd-jwt" *( ";" parameter ) ( "," sd-jwt-compact / ";base64," base64-data )

; "When including verifiable credentials secured with COSE in verifiable
; presentations as Enveloped Verifiable Credentials, the credentials MUST be
; encoded using base64 as specified in [RFC2397]." (w3c-vc-jose-cose#R-0051)
; Stated for credentials only (section 3.3.1): for application/vp+cose the
; source states no encoding, so both RFC 2397 forms are admitted; Example 23
; uses ;base64.
vc-cose-data-uri   = "data:application/vc+cose" *( ";" parameter ) ";base64," base64-data
vp-cose-data-uri   = "data:application/vp+cose" *( ";" parameter ) ( ";base64," base64-data / "," *uric )

; RFC 7515 section 7.1 compact serialization, written as RFC 9901's JWT
; rule. SD-JWT-KB is listed first; under RFC 5234 an alternation is
; unordered, and the two cannot both match one whole string, because a
; KB-JWT contains periods and a disclosure does not.
jws-compact    = BASE64URL "." BASE64URL "." BASE64URL
sd-jwt-compact = SD-JWT-KB / SD-JWT

; RFC 9901 section 4 (SD-JWT, November 2025), the six rules after its ALPHA
; and DIGIT, restated unchanged in rule names and definitions, for example
; "SD-JWT-KB = SD-JWT KB-JWT" (RFC 9901 section 4); comments and spacing are
; this file's. The source cites [SD-JWT] as an Internet-Draft with no
; revision (C.1); RFC 9901 is its published form. An SD-JWT+KB followed by a
; further tilde, as Example 22 prints, matches neither SD-JWT nor SD-JWT-KB.
BASE64URL  = 1*(ALPHA / DIGIT / "-" / "_")
JWT        = BASE64URL "." BASE64URL "." BASE64URL
DISCLOSURE = BASE64URL
SD-JWT     = JWT "~" *(DISCLOSURE "~")
KB-JWT     = JWT
SD-JWT-KB  = SD-JWT KB-JWT

; base64, RFC 2045 section 6.8 alphabet, padded to a multiple of four
; characters, no line breaks (a URL carries none). RFC 2397 says only that
; ;base64 means the data is encoded as base64; strict padding is inferred.
; base64url (- and _) is not this alphabet.
base64-data = *(4base64-char) [ 2base64-char "==" / 3base64-char "=" ]
base64-char = ALPHA / DIGIT / "+" / "/"

; RFC 2397 section 3 (August 1998), restated in ABNF from its BNF, rule
; names kept: for example 'parameter := attribute "=" value' (RFC 2397
; section 3). A parameter needs an =, which is how ;base64 is told apart, so
; ;base64url, which has none, is neither: Example 20's
; data:application/vc+cose;base64url,... matches no rule here.
dataurl    = "data:" [ mediatype ] [ ";base64" ] "," data
mediatype  = [ type "/" subtype ] *( ";" parameter )
data       = *urlchar
parameter  = attribute "=" value
type       = token
subtype    = token
attribute  = token
value      = token

; RFC 2397 takes type, subtype, attribute and value from RFC 2045 (token),
; "represented using URL escaped encoding of [RFC2396] as necessary." (RFC
; 2397 section 3): the RFC 2045 token characters that are also RFC 2396 uric
; characters, or an escape (inferred: neither RFC writes this intersection).
token      = 1*( ALPHA / DIGIT / "!" / "$" / "&" / "'" / "*" / "+" / "-" / "." / "_" / "~" / escaped )

; 'where "urlchar" is imported from [RFC2396]' (RFC 2397 section 3), and RFC
; 2396 defines no rule of that name; its uric (section 2), "uric = reserved
; | unreserved | escaped" (RFC 2396 section 2), is read for it (inferred). A
; space is not a uric, so a data: URL with a space in it matches nothing
; here; README.md, Findings, lists the examples that carry one.
urlchar    = uric
uric       = reserved / unreserved / escaped
reserved   = ";" / "/" / "?" / ":" / "@" / "&" / "=" / "+" / "$" / ","
unreserved = alphanum / mark
alphanum   = ALPHA / DIGIT
mark       = "-" / "_" / "." / "!" / "~" / "*" / "'" / "(" / ")"
escaped    = "%" HEXDIG HEXDIG
